#---
# purpose: "a guard helper whose body came from a quote resolves its retained callable like any other call"
#---
#
# # quoted_guard_helper_keeps_its_provider
#
# `Provider.make/0` splices a `small?/1` whose body is a quoted call to
# `Provider.base/1`. `small?/1` then guards a clause of `App.pick/1`.
#
# Guard reification reads a clause's calls to decide what the guard dispatches
# on, so it has to recognise a retained callable as a call. A reader that only
# knows source spellings sees no callee at all and reports the helper impure —
# a true statement about nothing, since `base/1` is a perfectly pure comparison.
#
defmodule Provider do
  def base(x), do: x < 10

  defmacro make() do
    quote do
      def small?(x) do
        base(x)
      end
    end
  end
end

defmodule App do
  import Provider, only: [make: 0]

  make()

  def pick(x) when small?(x), do: 1
  def pick(_x), do: 0
end

def main() do
  assert(App.pick(3) == 1, "the quoted helper guards the first clause")
  assert(App.pick(30) == 0, "and falls through when it misses")
end
